The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// The check for DMA mapping support on 32-bit systems with 64-bit DMA is restored
if (sizeof(dma_addr_t) > sizeof(unsigned long))
    return -EOPNOTSUPP;
```
Reason:
Restoring this check ensures that if the system is indeed a 32-bit architecture with 64-bit DMA addresses, it will correctly return an error when attempting to use DMA mapping. This prevents the Ethernet driver from using the page pool with the `PP_FLAG_DMA_MAP` flag in an unsupported configuration, thus fixing the bootup process issue.

